# Bring a hot-plugged CPU or memory block online, on this machine.
#
# The distribution ships a rule for exactly this, 40-vm-hotadd.rules, and it does nothing
# here: it opens by checking the DMI vendor for Microsoft Hyper-V or Xen and jumps past
# itself for anything else. This machine reports QEMU — the guest's own udev prints
# `value '[dmi/id]sys_vendor' is 'QEMU'` — so on every machine this image ever runs on, that
# rule is a no-op.
#
# Ungated, and not re-gated on QEMU, because a hypervisor test is exactly the mistake being
# fixed. A CPU that was added and left offline is a core somebody paid for and cannot use,
# and there is no arrangement in which the right answer is to leave it offline.
#
# Growing is what a workspace does: it starts at the smallest machine that can be restored
# from a template and is grown to the size that was asked for. Without this the host has to
# reach into the guest over RPC to online each core it just attached, which is a second
# mechanism doing what the guest's own event handling does, and one that stops being
# reachable the moment the guest's init hands over to something else.
SUBSYSTEM=="cpu", ACTION=="add", DEVPATH=="/devices/system/cpu/cpu[0-9]*", TEST=="online", ATTR{online}!="1", ATTR{online}="1"

# Memory arrives through virtio-mem rather than as ACPI DIMMs, and the kernel command line
# already carries memhp_default_state=online — so this is the belt to that braces: a block
# the kernel did not online for itself still gets onlined here.
SUBSYSTEM=="memory", ACTION=="add", DEVPATH=="/devices/system/memory/memory[0-9]*", TEST=="state", ATTR{state}!="online", ATTR{state}="online"
